---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What The Attaché Desk is: an editorial reference for diplomats, consular staff and international officials on posting."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="shell page-head__inner">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span><span>About</span>
      </nav>
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--on-navy">About the Desk</span>
      <h1>An editorial desk for diplomatic life.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        A practical reference for the people who move between postings,
        structured, current, and written in plain terms.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          The Attaché Desk was founded by Anthony Rocco, who has worked with
          the diplomatic and international official community since 2019. Over
          that time, one pattern recurred without fail: people arriving at a
          new posting with full agendas and incomplete information. The
          questions were always practical. Where do I register the car? Which
          schools have places? What does the status document actually cover?
          The answers existed, but not in one place, not in plain language, and
          not structured for someone with two weeks to get organised.
        </p>

        <h2>A note from the founder</h2>
        <p>
          I have spent several years working alongside diplomats and
          international officials, close enough to see what posting life
          actually looks like from the inside. What struck me, consistently,
          was not the ceremonial side of diplomatic life, though that world is
          genuinely fascinating, but the practical gap that opens up the
          moment someone lands in a new city with a title, a timeline, and a
          hundred unresolved questions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The gap rarely came from a lack of information. It came from
          information scattered across ministries, mission circulars and
          word of mouth, in a format nobody had time to piece together in
          their first fortnight.
        </p>
        <p>
          That gap is what this desk exists to close.
        </p>
        <p><em>Anthony Rocco, Founder</em></p>

        <h2>What it is</h2>
        <p>
          A reference resource, organised city by city. Each guide covers the
          four areas that create the most friction on arrival: residence and
          legal status, vehicles, international schooling, and the security
          environment. Guides are built to be scanned and acted on, not read
          from cover to cover. Alongside the longer guides,
          <a href="/the-brief">The Brief</a> carries shorter dispatches on
          relocation, protocol and the practical mechanics of life on posting.
        </p>

        <h2>What it is not</h2>
        <p>
          It is not a concierge service and not a relocation agency. It sells
          nothing and arranges nothing on your behalf. It is not a personal
          blog, and it is not a substitute for legal, tax or immigration
          advice. Where your status, entitlements or obligations are involved,
          your mission and the relevant official authorities remain the
          authority. This desk is orientation that helps you ask the right
          questions.
        </p>

        <h2>How it is written</h2>
        <p>
          Every guide is researched and written on the basis of verifiable
          official sources, which are cited at the bottom of each page. The
          standard is simple: if a fact cannot be sourced to an official
          authority, a government publication, or an established primary
          reference, it does not appear. Guides carry a last-reviewed date and
          a file reference so you can see how current they are. The full
          editorial method, including sourcing standards and review cadence,
          is set out on the <a href="/editorial-method">Editorial Method</a> page.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Accuracy matters more than speed here. If something is out of date
          or wrong, it should be corrected. Write to
          <a href="mailto:contact@theattachedesk.com">contact@theattachedesk.com</a>
          with the page concerned, the inaccuracy, and a reference to the
          correct current source where possible.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
